{{Short description|Annual ranking of wealthiest individuals in Okaiken}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=March 2026}}

This is a list of the wealthiest people in [[Okaiken]], compiled and published annually by the '''Okaiken Wealth Organization''' (OWO; {{nihongo|岡井財界組織|Okaiken Zaikai Soshiki}}), an independent research body established in 1987. The ranking was founded following the asset bubble era, which exposed massive wealth concentration among the [[zaibatsu]] conglomerates and revealed widespread fraud in self-reported wealth declarations.

Unlike similar rankings in other nations, the OWO list is distinguished by its rigorous verification standards mandated by the  {{nihongo|'''Wealth Disclosure Act of 1996'''|資産公開法|Shisan Kōhō Hō}}, which requires all individuals with net worth exceeding ¥50 billion (approximately ε500 million) to file detailed asset declarations with the [[Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance]]. This legislative framework was enacted following the '''Suzuki Accounting Scandal''' of 1993, which revealed that several self-reported billionaires had inflated their holdings by up to 340%.

As of the 2025 ranking, the combined net worth of the top 50 individuals totals approximately '''ε447.2 billion''' (¥44.72 trillion), representing roughly 11.1% of Okaiken's GDP. This figure represents a 3.2% decline from 2024, marking the third consecutive year of contraction attributed to the [[National Universal Health Act]] surcharges and increased inheritance tax enforcement under the [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda administration]].

==Methodology and legal framework==
{{see also|Wealth Disclosure Act (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)}}

The OWO ranking employs a comprehensive methodology that distinguishes it from similar international rankings:

===The 1996 Disclosure Act===
Following the collapse of the asset bubble and the exposure of widespread corporate fraud in the early 1990s, the Diet passed the '''Wealth Disclosure Act''' with bipartisan support. The legislation established:

* Mandatory annual filing for individuals with net assets exceeding ¥50 billion
* Criminal penalties (up to 5 years imprisonment) for willful misrepresentation
* Public accessibility of filings through the Ministry of Finance database
* Independent audit requirements for holdings exceeding ¥100 billion

The Act was championed by Finance Minister [[Kenzan Mihashi]] as part of his post-bubble "transparency reforms," though critics noted it was also designed to curb the political influence of the traditional zaibatsu families who had dominated Sanese politics since the [[Saito Restoration]].

===Verification process===
All declarations are cross-referenced with:
* [[Okaiken Stock Exchange]] holdings data
* Real estate registries maintained by prefectural governments
* Corporate disclosure filings with the [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]]
* International asset tracking through bilateral tax treaties with [[Zong]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Yanbaru]]

Discrepancies exceeding 5% trigger automatic audits by the Ministry's '''Special Investigations Division''' ({{nihongo|特別調査課|Tokubetsu Chōsa-ka}}), which has earned the nickname "the Oni of Okami" for its aggressive prosecution of tax evasion cases.

