==Notable profiles and controversies==

===Hotta Katsunori (Rank 1)===
{{main|Hotta Katsunori|Hotta Holdings}}

'''Hotta Katsunori''' (born 1954) has held the top position since 2018, when he inherited control of [[Hotta Holdings]] following the death of his father, [[Hotta Munehisa]], who built the family fortune through post-war construction and real estate development. Hotta Holdings is now a diversified conglomerate with interests in finance, logistics, and renewable energy.

====Succession crisis (2023–present)====
In 2023, Hotta's eldest son and designated heir, [[Hotta Munenari|Munenari]] (born 1982), was removed from the board of Hotta Holdings following a very public dispute over the company's proposed merger with [[Kizan Systems]]. Munenari had advocated for the merger as necessary for modernization, while his father insisted on maintaining traditional zaibatsu independence. The conflict escalated when Munenari filed a lawsuit alleging that his father was mentally unfit to manage the family holdings, citing "erratic decision-making" and "paranoid hoarding of assets."

The [[Okami Family Court]] appointed an independent guardian ''ad litem'' to investigate, and in a landmark ruling under the '''Family Assets Preservation Act''', ordered Hotta Katsunori to submit to quarterly cognitive evaluations as a condition of continued control over the family trust. The case remains ongoing, with the court-appointed mediator, Judge [[Yamamoto Keiko]], describing it as "the most complex succession dispute in modern Sanese corporate history."

====Regulatory scrutiny====
In 2024, the [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]] launched an investigation into Hotta Holdings' cross-shareholding arrangements with [[Matsudaira Financial Group]], alleging potential violations of the '''Antimonopoly Act''' and the '''Wealth Disclosure Act'''. The investigation was triggered by whistleblower testimony from former Hotta Holdings executive [[Kawashima Toshio]], who alleged that the company had understated its holdings in offshore accounts in [[Nakamizu]] by approximately ε2.3 billion. Hotta has denied all allegations, and the investigation is ongoing.

===Kizan Toshiaki (Rank 2)===
{{main|Kizan Toshiaki|Kizan Systems}}

'''Kizan Toshiaki''' (born 1968) is the founder and CEO of [[Kizan Systems]], the dominant semiconductor manufacturer in Okaiken and primary supplier to [[Okaiken Airways]] and the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]]. Unlike most individuals on the list who inherited family wealth, Kizan is a self-made billionaire who founded his company in 1991 during the "Technocratic Pivot" era under Prime Minister [[Kenshin Hirota]].

====Government contracts controversy====
Kizan Systems has been the subject of intense scrutiny following a 2022 investigation by the ''[[Okami Financial Chronicle]]'' that revealed the company had secured over ε890 million in government contracts without competitive bidding between 2018 and 2022. The contracts, primarily for defense-related semiconductor components, were awarded under the '''Emergency Procurement Act''' enacted during the [[Data-Gate]] scandal.

The [[Board of Audit (Okaiken)|Board of Audit]] launched a formal investigation in 2023, and in 2024, the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] Budget Committee summoned Kizan to testify under oath. During the hearing, Kizan acknowledged "procedural irregularities" but defended the contracts as necessary for national security. Opposition lawmakers from the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Socialist Party]] and [[Sanese Communist Party|Communist Party]] accused the government of favoritism, noting that Kizan had donated ¥500 million to the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] between 2015 and 2020.

In response, the Diet passed the '''Competitive Bidding Reform Act of 2024''', which requires all defense contracts exceeding ¥1 billion to undergo mandatory public tender with independent oversight. Kizan Systems' contracts are currently under review by the [[Ministry of Defense (Okaiken)|Ministry of Defense]].

====Family dispute====
In 2025, Kizan's younger brother, [[Kizan Masahiro]], filed a lawsuit alleging that Toshiaki had improperly excluded him from the family trust. Masahiro, who owns 8% of Kizan Systems shares, claimed that his brother had pressured their elderly mother into changing her will to leave all family assets to Toshiaki. The case is pending in [[Okami District Court]].

===Matsudaira Kenji (Rank 3)===
{{main|Matsudaira Kenji|Matsudaira Financial Group}}

'''Matsudaira Kenji''' (born 1961) is the chairman of [[Matsudaira Financial Group]], one of the largest banking conglomerates in Okaiken. The Matsudaira family built their fortune through banking and insurance operations established in the early 20th century, expanding significantly during the post-war economic boom.

====Bad loans scandal (2020–2022)====
Between 2020 and 2022, Matsudaira Financial Group was at the center of a major banking scandal when it was revealed that the bank had concealed approximately ¥1.2 trillion in non-performing loans to failing construction companies in [[Yagikoshu]]. The [[Bank of Okaiken]] conducted a special inspection and found that Matsudaira had systematically understated its loan-loss provisions to maintain its stock price.

The [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]] imposed a ¥45 billion fine and ordered Matsudaira to suspend its dividend payments for two years. Matsudaira Kenji personally accepted a 50% pay cut and publicly apologized, but resisted calls for his resignation. Three executives were arrested and sentenced to prison terms ranging from 18 months to 4 years.

The scandal led to the passage of the '''Banking Transparency Act of 2023''', which requires all banks with assets exceeding ¥10 trillion to undergo quarterly stress tests by the Bank of Okaiken.

===Kawashima Yuki (Rank 4)===
{{main|Kawashima Yuki|Kawashima Motors}}

'''Kawashima Yuki''' (born 1985) is the youngest member of the top 10 and the only woman in that tier. She inherited control of [[Kawashima Motors]] in 2019 following the death of her father, [[Kawashima Hiroshi]], in a private helicopter crash near [[Aizawa]]. The crash remains under investigation by the [[Transport Safety Board (Okaiken)|Transport Safety Board]].

====Inheritance tax dispute====
Kawashima's inheritance was immediately complicated by the largest inheritance tax assessment in Sanese history. The [[National Tax Agency (Okaiken)|National Tax Agency]] valued her inheritance at ε18.3 billion and demanded ε7.2 billion in taxes. Kawashima contested the assessment, arguing that the company's true value was significantly lower due to "undisclosed liabilities" and "market uncertainties."

The case went to the [[Okami High Court]], which in 2023 ruled partially in Kawashima's favor, reducing the assessed value to ε15.1 billion and the tax owed to ε5.9 billion. However, the court also found that Kawashima had failed to disclose certain offshore holdings in [[Yanbaru]], and imposed an additional ¥800 million penalty for "negligent filing."

====Labor relations====
Kawashima Motors has faced ongoing labor unrest since 2021, when the company announced plans to automate 30% of its workforce by 2030. The [[Sanese Federation of Trade Unions]] organized a series of strikes in 2022 and 2023, demanding job security guarantees and profit-sharing arrangements. In 2024, Kawashima agreed to a five-year no-layoff pledge in exchange for wage freezes, but the dispute has damaged her public image.

===Ogawa Mitsuko (Rank 5)===
{{main|Ogawa Mitsuko|Ogawa Pharmaceutical}}

'''Ogawa Mitsuko''' (born 1959) is the chairwoman of [[Ogawa Pharmaceutical]], the largest pharmaceutical company in Okaiken. She is the highest-ranking woman on the list and one of only three women in the top 10.

====Drug pricing investigation====
In 2024, Ogawa Pharmaceutical was investigated by the [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Health]] for allegedly inflating prices on generic medications covered by the [[National Universal Health Act]]. The investigation found that Ogawa had charged the national health insurance system approximately ¥23 billion more than the legally permitted maximum between 2021 and 2023.

The company agreed to a ¥35 billion settlement without admitting wrongdoing, and Ogawa Mitsuko personally paid an additional ¥2 billion "voluntary contribution" to the Health Ministry's research fund. The scandal led to the '''Pharmaceutical Price Control Act of 2025''', which established real-time price monitoring and automatic refunds for overcharges.

===Tachibana Masato (Rank 6)===
{{main|Tachibana Masato|Tachibana Group}}

'''Tachibana Masato''' (born 1967) is the CEO of [[Tachibana Group]], Okaiken's largest retail conglomerate operating over 12,000 convenience stores, supermarkets, and department stores nationwide. He inherited the company from his father in 1998 and expanded it aggressively through acquisitions during the Lost Decades.

====Inheritance dispute (2021–2024)====
In 2021, Tachibana's younger sister, [[Tachibana Yumi]], filed a lawsuit alleging that Masato had fraudulently induced their father to change his will on his deathbed, cutting her out of a 20% stake in the company. The case exposed bitter family divisions, with Yumi presenting evidence that their father had been suffering from dementia at the time of the will's signing.

The [[Okami District Court]] ruled in 2024 that the will was valid, but found that Masato had failed to properly disclose certain family assets to Yumi. The court ordered Tachibana Group to pay Yumi ¥8.2 billion in compensation for her share of undisclosed holdings. Masato has appealed the ruling.

====Labor practices investigation====
In 2023, the [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Labour]] launched an investigation into Tachibana Group's use of "disguised contractors"—workers classified as independent contractors to avoid providing benefits. The investigation found that approximately 35,000 workers had been misclassified, and the company was ordered to pay ¥12 billion in back benefits and penalties.

===Suzuki Masato (Rank 9)===
{{main|Suzuki Masato|Suzuki Heavy Industries}}

'''Suzuki Masato''' (born 1981) is the chairman of [[Suzuki Heavy Industries]], a major defense contractor and shipbuilder. His wealth declined by ε2.1 billion in 2024–2025, the largest drop among the top 10.

====Accounting fraud investigation====
In February 2025, Suzuki Heavy Industries announced that an internal investigation had uncovered "material accounting irregularities" dating back to 2018. The company estimated that it had overstated profits by approximately ¥180 billion over seven years through improper booking of defense contracts.

The [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]] immediately suspended trading of Suzuki shares and launched a criminal investigation. Suzuki Masato denied personal involvement, blaming the fraud on "rogue executives" in the defense division. Three executives were arrested in March 2025, and the company faces potential delisting from the [[Okaiken Stock Exchange]].

The scandal is expected to result in Suzuki's removal from the top 10 in the 2026 ranking, with analysts predicting a decline of at least ε5 billion in net worth.

===Takahashi Naomi (Rank 10)===
{{main|Takahashi Naomi|Takahashi Media Group}}

'''Takahashi Naomi''' (born 1987) is the youngest member of the top 10 and the only self-made woman in that tier. She founded [[Takahashi Media Group]] in 2012, which has grown into Okaiken's largest digital media conglomerate through aggressive acquisitions of traditional newspapers and television stations.

====Monopoly concerns====
In 2024, the [[Fair Trade Commission (Okaiken)|Fair Trade Commission]] launched an investigation into whether Takahashi Media Group had violated antitrust laws by acquiring 78% of all media outlets in the [[Okami]] metropolitan area. The investigation was prompted by complaints from the [[Sanese Journalists Association]], which alleged that Takahashi had used her market dominance to suppress critical coverage of her business activities.

The investigation is ongoing, but preliminary findings suggest that Takahashi Media Group may be forced to divest some holdings. Takahashi has denied any wrongdoing, arguing that her acquisitions were "necessary to preserve traditional media in the digital age."

