==Government accountability mechanisms==

===The Wealth Disclosure Act (1996)===
{{main|Wealth Disclosure Act (Okaiken)}}

The '''Wealth Disclosure Act of 1996''' ({{nihongo|資産公開法|Shisan Kōhō Hō}}) is the primary legal framework ensuring transparency among the wealthy elite. Key provisions include:

* '''Mandatory filing''': All individuals with net assets exceeding ¥50 billion must file annual declarations with the [[Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance]]
* '''Criminal penalties''': Willful misrepresentation is punishable by up to 5 years imprisonment and fines of up to ¥500 million
* '''Public access''': All filings are publicly accessible through the Ministry of Finance database
* '''Independent audits''': Holdings exceeding ¥100 billion must be audited by certified public accountants approved by the [[w:Japanese Institute of Certified Public Accountants|Sanese Institute of Certified Public Accountants]]

The Act has been credited with significantly reducing fraud and tax evasion among the wealthy. According to Ministry of Finance data, the total declared wealth of the OWO ranking increased by 23% between 1996 and 2000, suggesting that the Act captured previously hidden assets.

===The Special Investigations Division===
{{main|Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)#Special Investigations Division}}

The '''Special Investigations Division''' ({{nihongo|特別調査課|Tokubetsu Chōsa-ka}}) is the enforcement arm of the Wealth Disclosure Act. Established in 1997, the Division employs 127 investigators with powers to:

* Conduct unannounced audits of declared assets
* Seize documents and electronic records from businesses and financial institutions
* Issue subpoenas for testimony from accountants, lawyers, and family members
* Recommend criminal prosecution to the [[Public Prosecutor's Office (Okaiken)|Public Prosecutor's Office]]

The Division has earned the nickname "the Oni of Okami" ({{nihongo|大紙の鬼|Okami no Oni}}) for its aggressive tactics. Between 1997 and 2025, the Division has secured 43 convictions for wealth disclosure violations, with defendants receiving prison sentences ranging from 6 months to 7 years.

===Inheritance tax enforcement===
The [[National Tax Agency (Okaiken)|National Tax Agency]] has significantly increased enforcement of inheritance taxes since 2018, when the [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda administration]] increased the top inheritance tax rate from 50% to 55% for estates exceeding ¥6 billion. The Agency has also deployed advanced data analytics to identify offshore holdings and trusts used to evade inheritance taxes.

Between 2018 and 2025, the Agency collected ¥2.3 trillion in additional inheritance taxes from the top 50 families, including ¥580 billion from the [[Hotta family]] and ¥420 billion from the [[Matsudaira family]].

==Criticism and controversy==

===Political influence===
Critics argue that the OWO ranking remains overly influential in Sanese politics despite the transparency reforms. According to the ''[[Okami Financial Chronicle]]'', members of the list contributed ¥12.7 billion to political parties between 2020 and 2025, with 68% going to the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]].

The '''Political Funds Control Act''' limits individual donations to ¥1.5 million per year per politician, but wealthy individuals can circumvent these limits by donating to multiple party branches and political action committees. The [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] and [[Sanese Communist Party]] have repeatedly called for stricter donation limits, but reform efforts have stalled in the [[House of Officers]].

===Wealth inequality concerns===
The concentration of wealth among those on the list has been cited as evidence of growing inequality in Okaiken. According to the [[Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (Okaiken)|Ministry of Internal Affairs]], the top 0.01% of the population (approximately 9,000 individuals) controls 12.3% of the nation's wealth, up from 8.7% in 2000.

The [[Honda Shigetsuma|Honda administration]] has defended the current system, arguing that the wealth of those on the list is "earned through legitimate business activities" and that the government's role is to ensure "fair competition and transparent regulation, not wealth redistribution."

===Methodology disputes===
Some economists have criticized the ranking's methodology for potentially understating the wealth of individuals with significant offshore holdings. The list relies on declarations filed under the Wealth Disclosure Act, but critics argue that sophisticated tax structures can still be used to conceal assets from authorities.

In 2023, the ''[[Okami Financial Chronicle]]'' acknowledged that the list might undercount wealth by 5–10% due to "difficulties in tracking assets held through complex corporate structures in [[Nakamizu]] and [[Yanbaru]]."

